{"id": "Q019", "question": "How did the director of La La Land get his start, and what connects his college years to that film's music?", "information_need": "in_depth", "motivation": "intrinsic_learning", "prior_familiarity": "familiar", "scope": "multi_document", "answerable": true, "adversarial_kind": null, "required_sources": ["La_La_Land.html", "Damien_Chazelle.html"], "expected_sources": ["La_La_Land.html", "Damien_Chazelle.html"], "retrieved_sources": ["La_La_Land.html", "Damien_Chazelle.html", "Titanic_(1997_film).html"], "required_aspects": ["Damien Chazelle met composer Justin Hurwitz at Harvard, where they were roommates in Currier House and explored a musical concept in their senior thesis, the low-budget Guy and Madeline on a Park Bench about a Boston jazz musician.", "Both moved to Los Angeles in 2010 and continued writing what became La La Land, altering the location from Boston to Los Angeles.", "Hurwitz composed and orchestrated the songs and score for La La Land, with lyrics by Pasek and Paul except for 'Start a Fire'."], "response": "Damien Chazelle studied filmmaking at Harvard University and graduated in 2007; while there, he lived in Currier House with composer Justin Hurwitz, who became his frequent collaborator. The two first explored the idea that later became *La La Land* in their senior thesis, through a low-budget musical about a Boston jazz musician [Damien_Chazelle.html].\n\nThat college connection carried directly into *La La Land*’s music: the film’s score and songs were composed by Justin Hurwitz, Chazelle’s Harvard classmate, and the project was first conceptualized by Chazelle alongside Hurwitz while they were at Harvard [La_La_Land.html] [Damien_Chazelle.html].", "closed_book_response": "Damien Chazelle, the director of *La La Land*, got his start by making films very early and then going on to study at Harvard. Before *La La Land*, he made smaller projects and gradually built his way up through indie filmmaking.\n\nWhat connects 