[2026-09-16T15:44:29] Q042: I've heard people say Crash winning Best Picture was a bad decision — what does the record actually say about how that's been assessed since?
{"id": "Q042", "question": "I've heard people say Crash winning Best Picture was a bad decision — what does the record actually say about how that's been assessed since?", "information_need": "overview", "motivation": "personal_decision", "prior_familiarity": "familiar", "scope": "multi_document", "answerable": true, "adversarial_kind": null, "required_sources": ["78th_Academy_Awards.html", "Academy_Award_for_Best_Picture.html", "Brokeback_Mountain.html"], "expected_sources": ["78th_Academy_Awards.html", "Brokeback_Mountain.html", "Academy_Award_for_Best_Picture.html"], "retrieved_sources": ["Academy_Award_for_Best_Picture.html", "Brokeback_Mountain.html", "78th_Academy_Awards.html"], "required_aspects": ["Crash's win over Brokeback Mountain has come to be seen as one of the most notorious decisions in modern Academy history and a source of earnest bafflement from critics, with rankings by several publications calling Crash one of the worst or most controversial Best Picture winners.", "Kenneth Turan accused Academy members of homophobia and of benefitting from a non-groundbreaking choice, and after announcing the award, presenter Jack Nicholson was caught mouthing 'whoa' in apparent surprise.", "In a 2015 Hollywood Reporter poll of Academy members on controversial past decisions, Brokeback Mountain won the revote for Best Picture."], "response": "The record says that Crash beating Brokeback Mountain for Best Picture has been viewed very negatively in hindsight. The 78th Academy Awards page says the win over Brokeback Mountain has become “one of the most notorious [decisions] in modern Academy history” and a source of “earnest bafflement from critics” [78th_Academy_Awards.html]. It also says that several publications and critics have ranked Crash as one of the worst or most controversial Best Picture winners [78th_Academy_Awards.html].\n\nThe Brokeback Mountain page likewise says that after Brokeback Mountain lost Best Picture to Crash, “some critics accused the Academy of homophobia and for making a non-groundbreaking choice” [Brokeback_Mountain.html]. It names critics including Kenneth Turan and Nikki Finke as deriding the decision, while Roger Ebert defended Crash’s win by arguing “the better film won” [Brokeback_Mountain.html].\n\nSo, based on the documents, the later assessment is that the decision was widely controversial and is now often regarded as one of the Oscars’ biggest upsets rather than a broadly respected choice [78th_Academy_Awards.html] [Brokeback_Mountain.html].", "closed_book_response": "If you mean **the 2005 Oscars**, then yes: **Crash winning Best Picture has often been reassessed very unfavorably in hindsight**.\n\nWhat the broader record generally shows is:\n\n- **It was divisive even at the time.** Many critics and viewers thought *Brokeback Mountain* was the stronger film and expected it to win.\n- **Retrospective rankings usually place Crash near the bottom** of Best Picture winners. It’s commonly cited as one of the weakest, if not the weakest, winners in Oscar history.\n- **Academic and critical commentary** often treats it as a case study in the Academy rewarding a more “important-seeming” socially conscious film over a more widely admired one.\n- **Audience memory has not been kind to it** compared with the other nominees from that year, several of which are now regarded as much more enduring films.\n\nThat said, a fair assessment is:\n\n- It wasn’t seen as an outrageous upset by everyone at the time.\n- Some people still defend it for its structure and message, even if that defense is less common now.\n- “Bad decision” is a value judgment, but **the consensus over time has clearly tilted toward seeing it as an especially weak Best Picture choice**.\n\nSo the short version is: **the historical assessment has mostly gone against Crash**. It’s widely remembered as one of the more controversial and least respected Best Picture wins.", "correctness": "pass", "graded_correctness": "complete", "graded_correctness_score": 1.0, "covered_aspects": [1, 2, 3], "aspect_coverage": 1.0, "answer_relevance": "pass", "claims": [{"text": "Crash beating Brokeback Mountain for Best Picture has been viewed very negatively in hindsight.", "cited_source": null, "grounded": true, "citation_supported": null, "attributable_to_retrieval": false}, {"text": "The 78th Academy Awards page says the win over Brokeback Mountain has become “one of the most notorious [decisions] in modern Academy history” and a source of “earnest bafflement from critics.”", "cited_source": "78th_Academy_Awards.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Several publications and critics have ranked Crash as one of the worst or most controversial Best Picture winners.", "cited_source": "78th_Academy_Awards.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "After Brokeback Mountain lost Best Picture to Crash, some critics accused the Academy of homophobia and of making a non-groundbreaking choice in Crash.", "cited_source": "Brokeback_Mountain.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The Brokeback Mountain page names Kenneth Turan and Nikki Finke as critics who derided the decision.", "cited_source": "Brokeback_Mountain.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Roger Ebert defended Crash’s win by arguing that “the better film won.”", "cited_source": "Brokeback_Mountain.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Based on the documents, the decision was widely controversial and is now often regarded as one of the Oscars’ biggest upsets rather than a broadly respected choice.", "cited_source": null, "grounded": true, "citation_supported": null, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}], "claim_count": 7, "graded_groundedness_score": 1.0, "faithfulness": "pass", "citation_support_rate": 1.0, "chunk_attribution_score": 0.8571428571428571, "source_hit_at_k": 1, "source_recall_at_k": 1.0, "source_precision_at_k": 1.0, "source_recall_strict": 1.0, "all_required_present": true, "response_quote_count": 4, "quote_fidelity_rate": 1.0, "quote_attribution_accuracy": 0.25, "citation_count": 2, "citation_precision": 1.0, "unsupported_citation_rate": 0.0, "refusal_outcome": "n/a", "answer_latency_seconds": 2.7584761999896728, "judge_latency_seconds": 8.16178359996411, "total_latency_seconds": 14.705721099948278, "input_tokens": 157586, "output_tokens": 1009, "total_tokens": 158595, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.12273}
