[2026-09-16T15:45:22] Q074: Describe how Spielberg came to make Schindler's List and how it affected his later work.
{"id": "Q074", "question": "Describe how Spielberg came to make Schindler's List and how it affected his later work.", "information_need": "in_depth", "motivation": "intrinsic_learning", "prior_familiarity": "unfamiliar", "scope": "multi_document", "answerable": true, "adversarial_kind": null, "required_sources": ["Steven_Spielberg.html"], "expected_sources": ["Steven_Spielberg.html", "Academy_Award_for_Best_Director.html"], "retrieved_sources": ["Steven_Spielberg.html", "Brokeback_Mountain.html", "Ben_Kingsley.html"], "required_aspects": ["Spielberg waited ten years to make Schindler's List because he did not feel mature enough, wanting to embrace his heritage", "after the birth of his son Max, he said a spirit ignited in him and he became 'a Jewish dad'.", "Principal photography began March 1, 1993 in Kraków, Poland, while he supervised post-production on Jurassic Park", "he brought his wife and children to make the shoot bearable.", "The film was a commercial success and Spielberg used his percentage of the profits to start the Shoah Foundation, a non-profit archiving Holocaust survivor testimonies.", "It won seven Academy Awards, including Best Picture and his first Best Director."], "response": "Spielberg made *Schindler's List* in 1993 after waiting ten years because he did not feel “mature” enough to make the film earlier. He wanted to embrace his Jewish heritage, and after the birth of his son Max, he said “a spirit began to ignite in me, and I became a Jewish dad.” [Steven_Spielberg.html]\n\nThe film also had a major impact on his later work: Spielberg used his percentage of the profits to start the Shoah Foundation, a nonprofit that archives Holocaust survivor testimonies. [Steven_Spielberg.html] It also appears to have deepened the role of Holocaust and Jewish history in his work and public life, as the document notes that he later became more openly connected to Judaism and that *Schindler's List* was one of the films for which he received major honors. [Steven_Spielberg.html]", "closed_book_response": "Steven Spielberg came to make *Schindler’s List* in a rather indirect way. The project had been in development for years, with several filmmakers attached at different times, but Spielberg initially hesitated. He felt he might not be the right person to tell such a deeply serious Holocaust story, especially because he was then widely known for adventure, fantasy, and family-oriented films. He was also reportedly unsure whether he had the emotional readiness to confront the subject fully.\n\nWhat helped push him toward making it was his growing awareness of antisemitism and the need to preserve Holocaust testimony at a time when survivors were aging. He eventually decided that the story had to be told, and that his visibility as one of the world’s most prominent directors could help ensure it reached a huge audience. By making the film in black and white and with a restrained style, he sought to avoid sensationalizing the material and instead make it feel historically grounded and morally direct.\n\nThe experience had a major effect on his later work. On a personal level, it deepened his connection to his Jewish identity and led him to become much more active in Holocaust remembrance and education, including the creation of the Shoah Foundation to record survivor testimony. Artistically, *Schindler’s List* marked a turning point: after it, Spielberg’s films often became more serious, emotionally weighty, and historically conscious, even when he returned to genre filmmaking. You can see this in later films such as *Amistad*, *Saving Private Ryan*, *Munich*, and *Lincoln*, which reflect a stronger engagement with history, moral complexity, and human suffering.\n\nAt the same time, he did not abandon his earlier style entirely. Instead, *Schindler’s List* broadened his range and seemed to give him permission to move between spectacle and solemn historical drama. In that sense, it was both a personal reckoning and a creative watershed in his career.", "correctness": "pass", "graded_correctness": "complete", "graded_correctness_score": 1.0, "covered_aspects": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6], "aspect_coverage": 1.0, "answer_relevance": "pass", "claims": [{"text": "Spielberg made *Schindler's List* in 1993 after waiting ten years because he did not feel “mature” enough to make the film earlier.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "He wanted to embrace his Jewish heritage.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "After the birth of his son Max, Spielberg said, “a spirit began to ignite in me, and I became a Jewish dad.”", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "Spielberg used his percentage of the profits from *Schindler's List* to start the Shoah Foundation.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The Shoah Foundation is a nonprofit that archives Holocaust survivor testimonies.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The document says Spielberg later became more openly connected to Judaism.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}, {"text": "The document says *Schindler's List* was one of the films for which Spielberg received major honors.", "cited_source": "Steven_Spielberg.html", "grounded": true, "citation_supported": true, "attributable_to_retrieval": true}], "claim_count": 7, "graded_groundedness_score": 1.0, "faithfulness": "pass", "citation_support_rate": 1.0, "chunk_attribution_score": 1.0, "source_hit_at_k": 1, "source_recall_at_k": 1.0, "source_precision_at_k": 0.3333333333333333, "source_recall_strict": 1.0, "all_required_present": true, "response_quote_count": 1, "quote_fidelity_rate": 0.0, "quote_attribution_accuracy": 0.0, "citation_count": 1, "citation_precision": 1.0, "unsupported_citation_rate": 0.0, "refusal_outcome": "n/a", "answer_latency_seconds": 2.9966692000161856, "judge_latency_seconds": 6.49561799998628, "total_latency_seconds": 13.129811599996174, "input_tokens": 272545, "output_tokens": 1006, "total_tokens": 273551, "estimated_cost_usd": 0.20893575}
